{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "pitchzazz: real-time audio engineering walkthrough",
  "description": "Seven stops demonstrating real-time-systems understanding, DSP correctness debugging, and a controlled two-language performance comparison. Written to be readable stop-to-stop without live narration — each description is self-contained. Steps use both a line-number guess and a text `pattern` CodeTour can use to relocate the step if the surrounding file has changed since this tour was written.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "1. Three real-time-safety violations, and the fix that runs throughout this codebase",
      "file": "crates/pitch-cli/src/main.rs",
      "line": 13,
      "pattern": "The prototype this project is based on violated that rule three ways",
      "description": "This project is a clean rebuild of an earlier prototype (`rusty-tuner`, 2023) whose DSP worked but whose real-time plumbing broke the one rule that matters on an audio callback thread — never block, allocate, or take a lock — in three different ways in about ten lines of code:\n\n1. **Lock in the callback**: `Arc<Mutex<AudioProcessor>>.lock()` called directly inside both audio callbacks. If the worker thread held that lock when a callback fired, the callback blocked — worst case, priority inversion, since the lower-priority worker thread could be preempted while holding it.\n2. **A blocking channel in the callback**: `std::sync::mpsc::Sender::send()`, called from inside the callback. `std`'s mpsc is itself mutex/condvar-backed internally — the same problem wearing a different API.\n3. **Allocation in the callback**: `data.clone()` on every single callback invocation — a heap allocation on the audio thread, on the steady-state path, not an edge case.\n\nThe double irony: the thing being locked in violation #1 was a `ringbuf::HeapRb`, whose entire purpose is to give you a lock-free structure so you don't need a mutex (see stop 2). Wrapping it in `Arc<Mutex<_>>` instead of `.split()`-ing it into non-blocking Producer/Consumer halves pays for a lock-free structure and then puts a lock in front of it anyway — strictly worse than a plain `Vec` + mutex would have been.\n\nThe fix, used identically for both directions (mic → worker, worker → speakers) throughout this file: split a `ringbuf::HeapRb` into a `Producer`/`Consumer` pair; `push_slice`/`pop_slice` never block — if the buffer is full or empty they just copy fewer samples and return the count; all real DSP work happens on a plain worker thread with no deadline, free to allocate and log. Scroll down to `build_input_stream`/`build_output_stream` in this same file to see the fix in the actual callback closures — fixed-size stack buffers for the multichannel downmix/upmix, no `Vec` allocation anywhere on the hot path. Full writeup: `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`'s \"What the original prototype got wrong\" section."
    },
    {
      "title": "2. The `ringbuf` dependency audit — verifying the lock-free claim by reading the source, not the docs",
      "file": "crates/pitch-cli/src/main.rs",
      "line": 27,
      "pattern": "Splitting it is what makes it lock-free",
      "description": "Before trusting `ringbuf` to actually be lock-free (the entire reason it's used instead of a mutex-guarded `Vec`), its actual source was read directly — not just its documentation — at `~/.cargo/registry/.../ringbuf-0.3.3/src/`. What that audit found: `.split()` gives you a `Producer`/`Consumer` pair that communicate via atomic head/tail indices (`CachePadded<AtomicUsize>`, `Ordering::Acquire`/`Release`), not a mutex; `push_slice`/`pop_slice` copy into/out of the buffer via `as_uninit_slices()`, bounded by slice length, no allocation. The only `Mutex` usage anywhere in the crate is in its own test suite — demonstrating the *wrong* way to use it for a different concurrency model, which is exactly the mistake the original prototype made (see stop 1).\n\nThis is the template this codebase uses for any new dependency: before adding one whose code would run inside a real-time callback, find its actual source and check for `Mutex`/`RwLock`/allocation on the hot path — don't trust a crate's README claims. `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`'s \"Dependency audit\" section documents that every other third-party crate in this codebase (`pitch-detection`, `pitch_shift`, `rust-music-theory`, `clap`, `anyhow`) only ever runs on the worker thread or at startup, by construction — never from the callback closures."
    },
    {
      "title": "3. The scale-quantization bug: a subtle correctness bug, found and fixed robustly",
      "file": "crates/pitch-core/src/scale.rs",
      "line": 13,
      "pattern": "That was the actual reason this project stalled originally",
      "description": "The original prototype's pitch detection and pitch shifting both worked, but note-snapping was silently wrong for some scales — this is the actual reason that project stalled, not a real-time bug. Root cause: the prototype built a lookup table of in-scale MIDI notes by walking `Scale::intervals` and writing into a `Vec` pre-sized to `scale.notes().len()`. That silently assumed `intervals.len() == notes.len()`, which does not hold for every `ScaleType`/`Mode` combination in the `rust-music-theory` crate — depending on whether the octave-closing interval is included, the loop either wrote past the end of the vector or left trailing zeroed (bogus) entries.\n\nThe fix (this file) sidesteps the interval bookkeeping entirely: instead of reconstructing scale-degree MIDI numbers from intervals, `nearest_in_scale_midi` reads the scale's pitch classes directly from `Scale::notes()` — the crate's own source of truth — and does a simple outward semitone search from the target note. Fewer invariants to get right, and it's trivially testable against every scale type; see this file's test module for the actual regression tests, including one specifically named for the original bug class (notes far from the scale's \"home octave\" indexing out of range or matching garbage).\n\nWorth noting for the C++ side too: `cpp-plugin/Source/DSP/Scale.cpp` doesn't lean on any external music-theory library at all — it hardcodes semitone-offset interval arrays directly and searches outward the same way, so the original bug class (a `rust-music-theory`-specific interval/notes mismatch) can't recur there by construction, not just by convention."
    },
    {
      "title": "4. Decoupling the DSP's analysis block size from the host's callback size",
      "file": "crates/pitch-cli/src/main.rs",
      "line": 49,
      "pattern": "How many samples the pitch corrector analyzes per block",
      "description": "`PitchCorrector` requires a fixed block size, sized once at construction — the McLeod pitch detector's internal buffers are allocated for a specific size, and the original prototype rebuilt the detector on every single call rather than dealing with this, which this rebuild deliberately avoids. The problem: audio hosts do **not** guarantee a fixed callback buffer size, so a naive \"process whatever the callback handed you\" design breaks the moment the host's block size doesn't match what the detector was built for.\n\nThe fix, visible in this file's worker thread (search for `filled` and `analysis_buf` below `BLOCK_SIZE`'s declaration): the worker accumulates samples from the input ring buffer into a fixed-size `analysis_buf`, tracking how many slots are filled, and only runs one `PitchCorrector::process()` pass once it has exactly `BLOCK_SIZE` (2048) samples — regardless of how many samples arrived in any given callback. This decouples the DSP's fixed analysis size from whatever the driver hands the input callback, at the cost of the accumulation delay itself becoming a latency contributor (see stop 6's engine-comparison numbers, and `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`'s Latency section, for how that interacts with the phase vocoder's own analysis window). The C++/JUCE port applies the identical pattern — see `cpp-plugin/Source/DSP/CorrectorWorker.cpp`, which accumulates into `analysisBuffer` the same way against the same `BLOCK_SIZE`, decoupled from whatever block size the DAW host hands `processBlock`."
    },
    {
      "title": "5. The Phase 1 performance investigation: budget miss → root cause → fix → measured result",
      "file": "docs/PERFORMANCE_LOG.md",
      "line": 20,
      "pattern": "Initial live smoke test surfaces a real-time budget miss",
      "description": "This is the single most complete \"software performance optimization, backed by data\" story in the repo — four entries in this log, in order, each building directly on the last:\n\n1. **\"Initial live smoke test surfaces a real-time budget miss\"** (this stop's anchor): the very first live-mic run measured `PitchCorrector::process()` taking 28-35ms typically against a 21.3ms budget — 40-60% over, structurally slower than real-time, not an edge case.\n2. **\"Per-stage breakdown: it's entirely the shift stage\"**: rather than guessing, the corrector was instrumented to time detection/quantization/shifting separately. Quantization cost ~5-14us (negligible); detection ~2000-2900us; the phase-vocoder shift stage alone cost 28,000-35,000us — on every block, including silent ones, which was the tell that this was a fixed parameter problem, not a content-dependent algorithmic one.\n3. **\"Oversampling 32 → 8: ~4x reduction\"**: `OVER_SAMPLING` (an unmeasured leftover from the original prototype, controlling the phase vocoder's STFT hop density) was the prime suspect. Dropping it from 32 to 8 cut the shift stage to 6,500-11,300us — almost exactly the predicted 4x reduction — bringing most blocks comfortably under budget. (It was later re-evaluated against release-build costs and raised to 16, once a controlled Rust-vs-C++ comparison showed there was far more headroom than the original debug-build numbers suggested — see this log's \"OVER_SAMPLING re-evaluated\" entry.)\n4. **\"Measured pipeline latency, both engines: real numbers, not an estimate\"**: the CPU-cost fix above is a different question from total mic-to-speaker latency. An impulse-response probe (feed a single-sample click through the real ring-buffer/FIFO pipeline, find the output peak) measured the actual pipeline latency at exactly one phase-vocoder analysis window's worth of samples — 50.0ms for the Rust engine at every sample rate (by construction, its frame size is always `sampleRate * 50ms`), 42.7-46.4ms for the C++ engine (frame size rounded to the nearest power of two for `juce::dsp::FFT`). This *revised down* an earlier ~70-100ms analytical estimate that had wrongly assumed block-accumulation delay and the phase vocoder's window stack additively — they don't; whichever lookahead requirement is larger wins, and the phase vocoder's window is always the larger one in this design.\n\nThe throughline: measure before guessing, root-cause with per-stage data rather than pattern-matching, and re-verify a decision (the oversampling value) against a *different* build profile once one became available, rather than treating an earlier debug-build conclusion as permanent."
    },
    {
      "title": "6. The hot-swap architecture, and the controlled Rust-vs-C++ benchmark that debunked a 20-30x illusion",
      "file": "cpp-plugin/Source/DSP/PitchEngine.h",
      "line": 42,
      "pattern": "The interface every pitch-correction algorithm implements to be",
      "description": "`PitchEngine` is the interface every pitch-correction algorithm implements to be hot-swappable at runtime — this plugin is deliberately built as a playground for comparing pitch-manipulation approaches (Rust via FFI, native C++ phase vocoder, C++ TD-PSOLA today), not a fixed pipeline with one hardcoded algorithm. `PitchEngineRegistry.h`'s `availableEngines()` (name → factory function list) is what makes adding a fourth algorithm mean \"implement this interface, write one factory function, add one registry line\" — the editor's engine selector and `CorrectorWorker` both enumerate that list generically instead of hardcoding engine names.\n\nThe swap itself lives in `cpp-plugin/Source/DSP/CorrectorWorker.cpp`'s `requestEngineSwap()`: a new engine is handed off via a lock-free single-producer/single-consumer pointer handoff, picked up between blocks on the worker thread, never mid-`process()` call — the same lock-free discipline as the audio ring buffers, applied to swapping one owned object instead of a sample stream. The swap wasn't trivially correct, though: `docs/FINDINGS.md` #14 documents a real bug this surfaced — an instant swap discarded the outgoing engine's warmed-up internal phase-vocoder state and started the new engine cold, an audible-scale discontinuity that no unit test, PluginVal, or `auval` run would have caught, since each engine was individually correct in isolation. The fix (visible a bit further down in this same file, around `crossfadeEngine`) runs both engines in parallel for a few blocks after a swap and blends them — iterated three times against real measured numbers, not against a loosened test threshold, before landing on a passing result.\n\nThe benchmark comparison this architecture enables (`docs/COMPARISON.md`): a first-pass comparison looked like C++ was 20-30x faster than Rust per block — wrong, because the Rust numbers were from a debug build and the C++ numbers from Release. Controlled for build profile, block size, and identical input, the real numbers (microseconds per block, both optimized) are 461.5 vs. 371.4 at 44100Hz, 349.1 vs. 368.3 at 48000Hz, 307.0 vs. 375.6 at 96000Hz (Rust vs. C++) — each language wins at a different rate, all six numbers within noise of each other, both engines using under 2% of the real-time budget. The actual lesson isn't \"which language is faster,\" it's that a build-profile mismatch is an easy, large, and easy-to-miss source of a misleading benchmark."
    },
    {
      "title": "7. `docs/FINDINGS.md`: the consolidated bug ledger, and the cross-tool-coverage story",
      "file": "docs/FINDINGS.md",
      "line": 14,
      "pattern": "Quick-reference table",
      "description": "This file is the presentation-ready index across every bug found in this project — both engines, the toolchain, and the scaffolding — with what found it, the root cause, and the fix, all in one table. It's meant to be pointed at directly rather than re-narrated from memory during a walkthrough; scroll down from the anchor above to see all entries.\n\nOne story from this ledger worth calling out specifically, since it's direct evidence that a single validation tool is never enough: finding #11 (`juce::Thread`'s destructor asserting/UB if destroyed while still running, because `PitchzazzAudioProcessor`'s destructor never stopped the worker thread) passed PluginVal at strictness 5 cleanly — PluginVal's own host-simulation harness apparently doesn't exercise the \"destroy without calling `releaseResources()` first\" path. It was caught by Apple's `auval`, the actual validator Logic Pro/GarageBand use internally, run independently as a second check specifically *because* relying on one validator felt insufficient. See this file's \"Validation tools used, and what each one actually catches\" section for the full breakdown of what each tool in this project's validation stack (unit tests, PluginVal, `auval`, manual source audit) does and doesn't catch — the point isn't that any one of them is bad, it's that they catch genuinely different bug classes, and this project's bug ledger is evidence of that in practice, not just in theory."
    }
  ]
}
